{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Concerning his sin-offering, wherein he sinned.</b> Here Scripture changed its language — for in the case of wealth and poverty it says מֵחַטָּאתוֹ, and here, in extreme poverty, it says עַל חַטָּאתוֹ. Our Sages deduced from here: if he sinned while rich and set aside money for a ewe or a she-goat and became poor, he brings from part of it two turtledoves; if he set aside money for two turtledoves and became poor, he brings from part of it a tenth of an ephah; if he set aside money for a tenth of an ephah and became rich, he adds to it and brings the offering of a rich person. Therefore it says here עַל חַטָּאתוֹ." "<b>From one of these.</b> From one of the three atonements stated in the matter — whether in wealth, in poverty, or in extreme poverty. And what does Scripture come to teach? For one might think the stricter among them would be a ewe or she-goat, and the lighter ones would be birds, and the lightest of the light would be a tenth of an ephah — Scripture says מֵאַחַת מֵאֵלֶּה, to equate the lighter ones to the stricter ones for a ewe and she-goat if his means allow, and the stricter ones to the lighter ones for a tenth of an ephah in extreme poverty (Sifra)." "<b>And it shall belong to the priest like the meal-offering.</b> To teach concerning a sinner's meal-offering that its remnant is eaten — this is according to its plain sense. And our Sages interpreted: ״וְהָיְתָה לַכֹּהֵן״ — and if this sinner is himself a priest, it is like the rest of a priest's freewill meal-offering, which is entirely turned to smoke and not eaten (Sifra)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:05:06.771273317Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}